#version 450
// ports/vulkan/shaders/native_gdn_step.comp - the PERFORMANCE tier's native GDN / DeltaNet recurrence,
// ported from src/kernels/cuda/native_gdn.cu's `step` kernel (host wrapper `native_gdn_step`,
// native_gdn.hpp:18-20), replacing the legacy `gdn_step` (shaders/gdn_step.comp,
// src/kernels/cuda/gdn.cu:43-95).
//
// THE RULE (native_gdn.cu:46-81), one thread per (head, column) of the (S, h_v, S) state, in place:
//
//     g_val    = expf(gate[head])                                     // decay, formed ONCE
//     kv_col   = sum_i state[(i,h,head)] * k[src*S + i]               // the UNDECAYED state dotted with k
//     delta    = (v[head*S + col] - g_val * kv_col) * beta[head]
//     s_i      = g_val * s_i + k[src*S + i] * delta                   // decay and rank-1 update in ONE step
//     attn     = sum_i s_i * q[src*S + i]                             // readout against the UPDATED state
//     output[h*S + col] = attn * scale                                // the folded 1/sqrt(S) readout scale
//
// WHAT THE NATIVE BODY IS, vs the legacy kernel, stated rather than assumed.  The two RULES are
// MATHEMATICALLY THE SAME (`g * sum(state*k)` == `sum((g*state)*k)`), so no element has a different
// intended value; what differs is the ARITHMETIC and the DISPATCH:
//
//   * the DECAY is folded into the rank-1 update (`g*s + k*delta`) and the `sk` contract is taken against
//     the UNDECAYED state, then multiplied by g - where the legacy shader writes `s = state*dec` first and
//     then contracts.  Same value, a different rounding order.
//   * the `1/sqrt(S)` READOUT scale is FUSED into this kernel (`attn * scale`).  The legacy shader does
//     NOT scale its readout; the legacy branch applies that factor to q in a SEPARATE `scale_inplace`
//     launch (src/core/layer.cpp:276), so the native kernel replaces a two-dispatch sequence - that is the
//     bench's layer-level comparison (`native_gdn_step` vs `scale_inplace` + `gdn_step`).
//
// THE DECOMPOSITION, AND A MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED IT.  The native body uses ONE 32-LANE WARP per column
// (four state rows per lane in registers, reduced with `__shfl_xor_sync`).  This port BANS subgroup ops
// (Intel picks the SIMD width per kernel; see common/wg_reduce.glsl), so the two non-subgroup renderings
// of that warp are (a) one WORKGROUP per column with the barrier tree, or (b) one THREAD per column with a
// serial walk over the row axis - which is the decomposition the legacy `gdn_step` port already uses, and
// which is COALESCED (adjacent threads read adjacent `j`), whereas (a) is not (adjacent invocations own
// rows `i` and `i+1`, which are `h_v*S` floats apart).  (a) was built and MEASURED against the legacy
// kernel at this shape, `bench/README.md`: **1.564x the Arc, 8.33x the Ryzen iGPU and 43.5x llvmpipe** -
// 6144 workgroups each doing two 8-round barrier trees, at 32 cache lines per warp, is a large
// REGRESSION, not a wash.  So the shipped port keeps the coalesced one-thread-per-column decomposition
// (the legacy port's own note: the CUDA's warp/staging shape is a parallelism strategy, not the rule) and
// carries the native body's ARITHMETIC and its fused readout scale.  The case's oracle is still the
// native RULE transcribed in double.
//
// =========================================================================================================
// WHY THE ROW WALK IS UNROLLED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A SUMMATION-ORDER CHANGE (the second measurement that
// decided this shader).  The phase this kernel carries is 2,675 ms of a 9,786 ms prefill timeline (27.3%,
// GPU-bound by its own flush wait), and the shape is the reason: `groups_for(h_v*S)` offers the card
// **24 workgroups x 256 = 6,144 threads**, i.e. under two waves on this card's 4,096 fp32 lanes (32 Xe2
// cores x 128) and - at one workgroup per core - 8 of 32 cores with no workgroup at all.  Each thread then
// walks S=128 rows TWICE with a load->FMA chain.  The bench's own probe (`gdn_step_probe`, this port's
// harness) measures where the time goes: the SAME kernel and grid read **0.0345 ms** when the 3 MiB state
// is L2-resident and **0.0619 ms** when the working set is the engine's real 36 states (108 MiB, over the
// 24 MB L2), i.e. the kernel is memory-LATENCY-bound, not ALU-bound: 6,144 threads at one or two
// outstanding loads each is ~24-49 KB of memory-level parallelism, and at this card's DRAM latency that
// sustains ~16% of its 608 GB/s.  The fix that does NOT touch the arithmetic is to issue the NEXT row's
// loads before the FMAs that consume them: the loop is unrolled by `kGdnKU` with the SAME single
// accumulator, adding each term in the SAME ascending `i` order, so the sequence of floating-point
// operations is bit-for-bit the rolled loop's (`(kv + t0) + t1` never becomes `kv + (t0 + t1)`).
// THIS IS THE PROPERTY THAT MATTERS: the GDN recurrence is chaotic (the port measured an eigenvalue near
// -19 per token on an un-normalised fixture), so any association change would move the ids.  A parallel
// scan was considered and REJECTED on exactly this ground - it is associative in exact arithmetic but not
// in f32, so it changes the answer.  The unroll does not, and the gate's `case_native_gdn_step` and the
// engine's id hashes are the guards.
// =========================================================================================================
//
// The head pairing is `src = h % h_k` (MODULO), the state layout is the port's (S, h_v, S) with j fastest
// (`state[(i*h_v + h)*S + j]`).  The dispatch is one thread per column, `groups_for(h_v * S)`; the column
// guard makes an over-dispatch harmless.  No barrier, no subgroup op, no atomic.
//
// THE UNROLL FACTOR, MEASURED ON THREE IMPLEMENTATIONS (`gdn_step_unroll`, batch 128, cold 36-state
// footprint / hot single state): KU=8 vs KU=16 are BOTH bit-exact against the rolled baseline (0/786,432
// state and 0/6,144 `o` elements differ on intel, lvp AND radeon).  Threads: intel cold 0.0618 -> 0.0231
// (KU=8) -> 0.0221 (KU=16); lvp cold 0.5856 -> 0.4559 (KU=8) / 0.4531 (KU=16); **radeon cold 0.4170 ->
// 0.4376 (KU=8) / 0.4167 (KU=16)** - the RADV iGPU is a wash at KU=8 and NEUTRAL at KU=16.  So KU=16 is the
// shipped factor: it is the faster one on the target and the one that does not regress the third
// implementation.  `-DKU=<n>` overrides it for the bench.
#ifndef KU
#define KU 16
#endif
layout(local_size_x = 256) in;
layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) buffer STATE { float v[]; } state;     // (S, h_v, S), j fastest, in place
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, std430) readonly buffer Q { float v[]; } qb;   // (h_k, S)
layout(set = 0, binding = 2, std430) readonly buffer K { float v[]; } kb;   // (h_k, S)
layout(set = 0, binding = 3, std430) readonly buffer V { float v[]; } vb;   // (h_v, S)
layout(set = 0, binding = 4, std430) readonly buffer G { float v[]; } gb;   // (h_v,) pre-exp gate
layout(set = 0, binding = 5, std430) readonly buffer B { float v[]; } bb;   // (h_v,)
layout(set = 0, binding = 6, std430) buffer O { float v[]; } ob;            // (h_v, S)
layout(push_constant, std430) uniform PC { int S; int h_k; int h_v; float scale; } pc;

void main() {
    const uint S = uint(pc.S), hv = uint(pc.h_v);
    const uint col = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;           // j fastest, then h: col = h*S + j
    if (col >= S * hv) return;
    const uint h = col / S;
    const uint j = col % S;
    const uint src = h % uint(pc.h_k);                  // MODULO head pairing (not h / (h_v/h_k))
    const float g = exp(gb.v[h]);                       // the native body's `g_val = expf(gate[head])`
    const float b = bb.v[h];

    // pass 1: the contract against the UNDECAYED state (the native body's `kv_col`).  ONE accumulator, terms
    // added in ascending `i` order; the KU loads of a chunk are issued together, before the FMAs consume them.
    float kv = 0.0f;
    uint i = 0u;
    for (; i + uint(KU) <= S; i += uint(KU)) {
        float ss[KU], kk[KU];
        for (uint u = 0u; u < uint(KU); ++u) {
            ss[u] = state.v[(uint(i + u) * hv + h) * S + j];
            kk[u] = kb.v[src * S + i + u];
        }
        for (uint u = 0u; u < uint(KU); ++u) kv += ss[u] * kk[u];
    }
    for (; i < S; ++i) kv += state.v[(i * hv + h) * S + j] * kb.v[src * S + i];
    const float delta = (vb.v[h * S + j] - g * kv) * b;
    // pass 2: the native body's single folded decay+rank-1 update, then the readout against the UPDATED state.
    // Same ascending order and the same single `attn` accumulator; the stores are independent of each other
    // and of the accumulation (each term is formed from a load, never from the previous row's result).
    float attn = 0.0f;
    i = 0u;
    for (; i + uint(KU) <= S; i += uint(KU)) {
        float ss[KU], kk[KU], qq[KU];
        uint idx[KU];
        for (uint u = 0u; u < uint(KU); ++u) {
            idx[u] = (uint(i + u) * hv + h) * S + j;
            ss[u] = state.v[idx[u]];
            kk[u] = kb.v[src * S + i + u];
            qq[u] = qb.v[src * S + i + u];
        }
        for (uint u = 0u; u < uint(KU); ++u) {
            const float s = g * ss[u] + kk[u] * delta;  // g*s + k*delta, one step
            state.v[idx[u]] = s;
            attn += s * qq[u];
        }
    }
    for (; i < S; ++i) {
        const uint idx = (i * hv + h) * S + j;
        const float s = g * state.v[idx] + kb.v[src * S + i] * delta;
        state.v[idx] = s;
        attn += s * qb.v[src * S + i];
    }
    ob.v[h * S + j] = attn * pc.scale;                  // the native body's folded 1/sqrt(S) readout scale
}
